---
source: crates/cli/tests/lint.rs
expression: out(&one)
---
sorted-keys
  languages:     all
  category:      style
  default level: off
  fix:           unsafe
  presets:       yaml/yamllint
  equivalent:    Biome assist/source/useSortedKeys (partial) https://biomejs.dev/assist/actions/use-sorted-keys/
                 eslint-plugin-jsonc jsonc/sort-keys (partial) https://ota-meshi.github.io/eslint-plugin-jsonc/rules/sort-keys.html
                 taplo reorder_keys (partial)
                 taplo reorder_inline_tables (partial)
                 yamllint key-ordering (partial) https://yamllint.readthedocs.io/en/stable/rules.html#module-yamllint.rules.key_ordering
                 yamllint key-ordering.ignored-keys https://yamllint.readthedocs.io/en/stable/rules.html#module-yamllint.rules.key_ordering

What it does
  Reports the keys of a mapping that are not in order (alphabetical by default). By default the fewest keys that would have to move are reported (`report: moved`), `misplaced` reports every key that sorts before one above it. The first report carries the fix. Comments on lines of their own stay where they are and a comment after an entry moves with it. Options: `order` (keys that come first), `direction`, `compare` (`ordinal`, `case_insensitive`, `natural`), `ignored_keys`, `min_keys` (default 2), `blank_line_groups`, `path_patterns` and `has_properties` (only mappings that have all of these keys). The fix is unsafe: moving a YAML anchor below its alias breaks the document.

Why it matters
  Keys in a fixed order are quick to look up, and two branches that add different keys to a sorted file rarely conflict. The order of a mapping is usually not data, but where a tool reads it (a build order, a dependency list that is applied top down) the rule should be limited with `path_patterns` or turned off.

  To fix, sort the keys; the fix does it.

Options:
  order (a list of strings, default []): Keys that come first, in this order; the rest follow in the configured order.
  direction (a string, default "asc"): `asc` or `desc`.
  compare (a string, default "ordinal"): `ordinal` (code points), `case_insensitive`, or `natural` (digit runs compare as numbers: `a2` before `a10`).
  ignored_keys (a list of strings, default []): Regular expressions; matching keys stay where they are and are not compared.
  min_keys (an integer, default 2): Mappings with fewer keys are not checked.
  blank_line_groups (a boolean, default false): Sort each run of entries between blank lines on its own.
  path_patterns (a list of strings, default []): Regular expressions; only mappings (sequences) whose key path matches one are checked. The path is `a.b[0].c` (`["odd key"]` for keys that are not identifiers), empty for the root; none listed means everywhere. A TOML table counts as its header key and an array table is not indexed.
  has_properties (a list of strings, default []): Only mappings that have all of these keys are checked.
  report (a string, default "moved"): Which keys are reported. `moved`: the fewest keys that would have to move (eslint-plugin-jsonc). `misplaced`: every key that sorts before one above it (yamllint).

Example:
  { "b": 1, "a": 2 }   // becomes { "a": 2, "b": 1 }
